1
00:00:03,224 --> 00:00:08,225
[SPEAKER_00]: made, just what your family did, where you grew up, you know, and what your dad did and so on?

2
00:00:08,585 --> 00:00:09,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, my dad was a farmer.

3
00:00:09,925 --> 00:00:17,587
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up about two and a half miles from here down at Easton, which is a little crossroads and a highway and, well, 25 houses or so.

4
00:00:18,507 --> 00:00:25,489
[SPEAKER_00]: And I lived there from the time I was about eight years old until I went away to play baseball.

5
00:00:26,269 --> 00:00:30,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was in 1930 was the first year I went to play baseball.

6
00:00:30,610 --> 00:00:31,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So I was just

7
00:00:34,131 --> 00:00:36,593
[SPEAKER_00]: play any ball on the farm or nearby?

8
00:00:36,613 --> 00:00:37,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sure.

9
00:00:37,113 --> 00:00:39,014
[SPEAKER_00]: I played even in grade school.

10
00:00:39,034 --> 00:00:39,694
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to that.

11
00:00:39,774 --> 00:00:41,175
[SPEAKER_00]: There was no other sports.

12
00:00:41,195 --> 00:00:46,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Football was hardly known then in our area, and basketball as far as that goes.

13
00:00:46,338 --> 00:00:52,421
[SPEAKER_00]: In grade school all we had was just the baseball and every chance of recession and everything.

14
00:00:52,601 --> 00:00:53,442
[SPEAKER_00]: The weather was decent.

15
00:00:53,462 --> 00:00:54,262
[SPEAKER_00]: We were out playing.

16
00:00:54,402 --> 00:00:55,223
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we played.

17
00:00:56,283 --> 00:01:01,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So we played all the variations of the games where there wasn't organized.

18
00:01:01,906 --> 00:01:02,807
[SPEAKER_00]: By that I mean the

19
00:01:03,572 --> 00:01:11,896
[SPEAKER_00]: the rotation where you start in right field and work your way around until you get up to bat and you make it out, and you just have to circle around and go through again.

20
00:01:12,616 --> 00:01:13,696
[SPEAKER_00]: And we played that.

21
00:01:14,196 --> 00:01:17,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, we had a couple of games with other grade schools at that time.

22
00:01:17,978 --> 00:01:19,619
[SPEAKER_00]: We were in the one-room schoolhouse.

23
00:01:19,679 --> 00:01:20,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Where was the school?

24
00:01:20,919 --> 00:01:24,981
[SPEAKER_00]: At Eastern, number 10 schoolhouse in Chippewa Township.

25
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:28,803
[SPEAKER_00]: And number three was about two miles up the road, up the hill a ways.

26
00:01:28,843 --> 00:01:32,044
[SPEAKER_00]: And we had games with them, and we played

27
00:01:32,695 --> 00:01:39,898
[SPEAKER_00]: The number four, which is about two miles over in another direction, that's about the limit of our... it's walking distance.

28
00:01:40,979 --> 00:01:42,120
[SPEAKER_00]: How big was the family?

29
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,742
[SPEAKER_00]: My family's two brothers, so it was just the five of us.

30
00:01:46,762 --> 00:01:48,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they older than you?

31
00:01:48,983 --> 00:01:50,244
[SPEAKER_00]: They're older than I am, yes.

32
00:01:50,284 --> 00:01:51,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they ballplayers?

33
00:01:51,224 --> 00:01:53,285
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

34
00:01:53,785 --> 00:01:57,387
[SPEAKER_00]: One of them wasn't interested in it at all, and the other one was very little.

35
00:01:58,864 --> 00:02:00,525
[SPEAKER_00]: They enjoy watching it, but that's playing.

36
00:02:00,545 --> 00:02:03,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they played around in the neighborhood, too, in those games.

37
00:02:03,126 --> 00:02:11,811
[SPEAKER_00]: But as far as going out and really trying to make even an amateur team at the town level, they weren't interested in that.

38
00:02:12,811 --> 00:02:17,393
[SPEAKER_00]: This one-room school, what grade did you go through there?

39
00:02:17,413 --> 00:02:20,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it was a one to eight, one through eight.

40
00:02:20,355 --> 00:02:26,658
[SPEAKER_00]: But I moved in there, I was eight years old, but I was already in the fourth grade at that time.

41
00:02:27,158 --> 00:02:27,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So I had

42
00:02:29,047 --> 00:02:40,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, over at another school where I lived, I was born in the rural route Marshall, Ohio, which is about two miles or three miles from where I was raised, finally.

43
00:02:41,334 --> 00:02:52,299
[SPEAKER_00]: And at that school, I was the youngest in the family, and one spring, when I was five years old, why, the teacher didn't mind that I went to school with my brothers for the last five weeks of school.

44
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:56,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I absorbed enough that the next year I started in the first grade, and by the time

45
00:02:57,593 --> 00:03:02,895
[SPEAKER_00]: That year was over, I was doing some third grade work, but I only skipped the second grade.

46
00:03:02,995 --> 00:03:11,197
[SPEAKER_00]: I went on then, and next year was in the third grade, and of course by the time I was eight, I was in the fourth grade, and we moved over to the other place.

47
00:03:12,578 --> 00:03:13,998
[SPEAKER_00]: How about high school grades?

48
00:03:14,038 --> 00:03:15,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Was this another school?

49
00:03:15,899 --> 00:03:20,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Dawnstown was where I was, and I went through Dawnstown here, four grades, four years.

50
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:25,522
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still living well, and on the farm, or did you move here?

51
00:03:26,216 --> 00:03:28,337
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I lived on the farm when I went to high school, yes.

52
00:03:28,377 --> 00:03:29,178
[SPEAKER_00]: How'd you commute?

53
00:03:30,298 --> 00:03:31,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Shanks, Mayors.

54
00:03:31,499 --> 00:03:31,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah?

55
00:03:31,879 --> 00:03:32,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

56
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:33,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Walking.

57
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,221
[SPEAKER_00]: You kept in shape that way, huh?

58
00:03:35,561 --> 00:03:38,523
[SPEAKER_00]: See, there was no such thing as a school bus then.

59
00:03:38,543 --> 00:03:39,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You just walked.

60
00:03:39,323 --> 00:03:41,164
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd get up and...

61
00:03:41,705 --> 00:03:48,989
[SPEAKER_00]: I played in various sports, and after school I would practice, and it might be after dark, so I was walking down the road going home.

62
00:03:49,529 --> 00:03:50,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a school bus?

63
00:03:51,130 --> 00:03:53,031
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I didn't do much of that.

64
00:03:53,491 --> 00:03:53,951
[SPEAKER_00]: I found...

65
00:03:54,031 --> 00:03:55,212
[SPEAKER_00]: I always found time to...

66
00:03:56,368 --> 00:04:01,209
[SPEAKER_00]: to, during the study periods, to do what homework I might have needed to do in order to pass.

67
00:04:01,269 --> 00:04:06,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, not to get great grades, I don't mean that, but enough to pass so I didn't have to take anything home.

68
00:04:06,130 --> 00:04:07,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Sounds familiar.

69
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:07,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

70
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:13,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Did they add all the sports that you also had in high school?

71
00:04:13,311 --> 00:04:13,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the major sports?

72
00:04:13,831 --> 00:04:16,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the sports, we had the three major sports, yes.

73
00:04:16,251 --> 00:04:18,452
[SPEAKER_00]: We had football, basketball, and baseball.

74
00:04:18,632 --> 00:04:20,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And what year were you there, your last year?

75
00:04:20,252 --> 00:04:22,292
[SPEAKER_00]: 1925, graduated in 1928.

76
00:04:25,852 --> 00:04:26,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Just 16 years old.

77
00:04:27,133 --> 00:04:28,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you play in all the teams?

78
00:04:30,194 --> 00:04:31,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't want to sound...

79
00:04:31,895 --> 00:04:33,696
[SPEAKER_00]: I went out for all the teams.

80
00:04:34,436 --> 00:04:39,559
[SPEAKER_00]: In the first two years, I was lucky to play... Well, I played on the baseball team, but I was real small.

81
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:42,620
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can believe that.

82
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:43,581
[SPEAKER_00]: What was your size?

83
00:04:44,862 --> 00:04:46,562
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, but I really...

84
00:04:46,582 --> 00:04:55,547
[SPEAKER_00]: I must have been about 5'2 or so up until the summer of my sophomore junior year.

85
00:04:56,193 --> 00:05:04,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And I can remember yet, I went back, one of my favorite teachers, who was also the principal, I spoke to her in the fall when I went back, and she didn't know me.

86
00:05:04,557 --> 00:05:09,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had been in all, in a lot of her classes, because I'd grown about eight or nine inches that summer.

87
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:11,181
[SPEAKER_00]: That's so I was told.

88
00:05:12,141 --> 00:05:17,384
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't remember it, it was kind of a thing that you don't remember yourself, because you don't realize, rather.

89
00:05:18,724 --> 00:05:23,427
[SPEAKER_00]: But I, then I got to where I was about six feet tall.

90
00:05:24,638 --> 00:05:26,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I grew some after I got out of school.

91
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:29,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Put on some more weight, of course.

92
00:05:29,901 --> 00:05:32,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then you probably didn't make the team the first year.

93
00:05:33,423 --> 00:05:34,603
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't make the football team.

94
00:05:34,823 --> 00:05:36,324
[SPEAKER_00]: The second year, I got to play a little bit.

95
00:05:36,364 --> 00:05:38,185
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get a letter, but I got a numeral.

96
00:05:38,905 --> 00:05:40,826
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess everybody that went out got numerals.

97
00:05:42,367 --> 00:05:45,009
[SPEAKER_00]: In basketball, I was on the squad.

98
00:05:45,189 --> 00:05:49,971
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't make the first squad and the second, just a reserve group for the first and second years.

99
00:05:50,011 --> 00:05:53,353
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the third and fourth years, I played in all sports, played regular.

100
00:05:53,882 --> 00:05:57,964
[SPEAKER_00]: a very good coach in baseball, sir.

101
00:05:58,664 --> 00:06:07,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Looking back, I would have to say it wasn't too great, because at that time it seemed like he was older and he was adult and knew more than I did and ran the game.

102
00:06:07,888 --> 00:06:09,548
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's right.

103
00:06:10,089 --> 00:06:11,629
[SPEAKER_00]: As good as anybody around had, sure.

104
00:06:12,209 --> 00:06:18,272
[SPEAKER_00]: And in fact, I should say this, as good as a lot of the high school coaches are at the present day.

105
00:06:18,777 --> 00:06:27,343
[SPEAKER_00]: because fundamentally, and on techniques and everything, a lot of them are just assistant basketball coaches, assistant football coaches that take this to get a little more money.

106
00:06:28,003 --> 00:06:30,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And go ahead and just put the team, say, team, go to work.

107
00:06:31,245 --> 00:06:37,429
[SPEAKER_00]: I saw a game, scouting around, and I saw a high school team with a coach laid on the bench between the innings, just laid down.

108
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:40,211
[SPEAKER_00]: This team was out playing, if you can imagine that.

109
00:06:40,291 --> 00:06:43,554
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't last long.

110
00:06:43,874 --> 00:06:45,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Next year, he wasn't a coach anymore.

111
00:06:48,452 --> 00:06:52,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you learn much from the coach or from the high school experience?

112
00:06:53,334 --> 00:06:56,815
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I learned mostly just from playing, and playing all the time I could.

113
00:06:57,115 --> 00:07:17,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Every chance I had where I'd play, and of course, before I ever joined any organized group, just my brother and I would come in from the fields for noon, and when dinner wasn't quite ready, dinner, that's a noonday meal, of course, on the farm, supper's the evening meal, why, we would play catch until dinner was ready.

114
00:07:18,078 --> 00:07:22,261
[SPEAKER_00]: And I would go and throw, and I learned to throw curve, of course, the wrong way.

115
00:07:22,281 --> 00:07:26,123
[SPEAKER_00]: I could throw a curveball real good, but I had my thumb over here like that, and it would go up there.

116
00:07:26,603 --> 00:07:37,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And like, I guess, most amateurs today, they like to see, they like to throw a curve that's flat, what we call a flat curve, like sidearm, because they can see it break.

117
00:07:38,391 --> 00:07:41,873
[SPEAKER_00]: What they don't stop to realize is, so can the hitter see it break better.

118
00:07:42,474 --> 00:07:46,116
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's better to throw it over, you don't notice it, and he doesn't notice it so much in time.

119
00:07:46,376 --> 00:07:47,817
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't get as good a view of it.

120
00:07:48,710 --> 00:07:51,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So I could throw that side on first.

121
00:07:51,931 --> 00:08:01,274
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I hear stories from folks that grew up on farms and they've seen pretty big pictures that they practice throwing rocks through tires and tin cans.

122
00:08:01,334 --> 00:08:02,815
[SPEAKER_00]: I never practiced anything like that.

123
00:08:02,875 --> 00:08:12,298
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to, of course, follow the team of horses a lot on the farm, harrowing and cultivating and doing the spring tooth and mowing and all that.

124
00:08:12,618 --> 00:08:14,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, especially the harrowing and spring toothing.

125
00:08:17,577 --> 00:08:20,859
[SPEAKER_00]: horses get a little lazy, one of them get a little lazy while you pick up a plot and throw them.

126
00:08:20,879 --> 00:08:27,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, you do that quite a bit, throw it, hit the horse to get him up there with the others.

127
00:08:28,084 --> 00:08:37,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, in driving the cattle out to pasture in the morning and evening, one of them, you stray a little while you throw it, then you straighten him out.

128
00:08:38,071 --> 00:08:39,752
[SPEAKER_00]: We used to do that quite a bit.

129
00:08:41,373 --> 00:08:45,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Bob Fowler, we used to practice throwing tires through it.

130
00:08:46,152 --> 00:08:49,754
[SPEAKER_00]: He retired to his dad at the barn, out back of the barn.

131
00:08:49,774 --> 00:08:50,715
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess that's all right.

132
00:08:50,735 --> 00:08:52,296
[SPEAKER_00]: I just never did that.

133
00:08:52,376 --> 00:08:56,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he didn't really need to, I guess, when he had something, others to play with.

134
00:08:56,899 --> 00:08:57,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's true.

135
00:08:57,539 --> 00:08:58,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Some teams to play in.

136
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:02,182
[SPEAKER_00]: And the technique wasn't developed.

137
00:09:02,222 --> 00:09:02,822
[SPEAKER_00]: We just played.

138
00:09:02,842 --> 00:09:03,763
[SPEAKER_00]: We just threw that way.

139
00:09:03,803 --> 00:09:14,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, my greatest eye-opener in my, as long as I played, was when I, first year I went to spring training with a professional club.

140
00:09:15,782 --> 00:09:30,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, as we worked out at home in amateur ball, everybody, we'd hit and fool around and catch a few grounders and a few fly balls, just fooling around, stand around and watch the other hitters and run the balls down as they hit.

141
00:09:30,653 --> 00:09:32,414
[SPEAKER_00]: And then when it was our turn to hit, we'd come in.

142
00:09:32,995 --> 00:09:37,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, my first spring training in pro ball, they started running us around the park.

143
00:09:37,358 --> 00:09:39,079
[SPEAKER_00]: We just put the gloves and the balls down.

144
00:09:39,099 --> 00:09:41,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Now we're going to run a while and get our legs in shape.

145
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:45,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And I say to this day that there aren't very many colleagues

146
00:09:46,513 --> 00:09:52,755
[SPEAKER_00]: are high school players that are in good condition to play baseball at the best of their ability.

147
00:09:53,855 --> 00:09:55,296
[SPEAKER_00]: For that reason, they're just not in shape.

148
00:09:55,536 --> 00:09:57,737
[SPEAKER_00]: And it isn't the fault of the coaches so much.

149
00:09:58,537 --> 00:10:01,598
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that they just don't have time to do all that work in school.

150
00:10:01,618 --> 00:10:03,378
[SPEAKER_00]: They've got studies come first.

151
00:10:03,739 --> 00:10:05,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Fast automobiles that drive to school.

152
00:10:05,819 --> 00:10:07,540
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another thing that's bad for the legs.

153
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody's riding every place.

154
00:10:10,141 --> 00:10:12,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Go down the corner, get a jump in the car, and go down.

155
00:10:12,701 --> 00:10:14,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me just check, see if we're coming in all right.

156
00:10:16,156 --> 00:10:19,877
[SPEAKER_00]: OK. Now, you graduated in 1928.

157
00:10:19,977 --> 00:10:24,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you like school?

158
00:10:25,779 --> 00:10:26,759
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked different sports.

159
00:10:27,559 --> 00:10:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked certain subjects, of course.

160
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:31,541
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked anything to do with the sciences.

161
00:10:32,301 --> 00:10:36,262
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked biology, and I liked popular science.

162
00:10:36,542 --> 00:10:41,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Was this in connection with your life on the farm and your interest in animals?

163
00:10:41,664 --> 00:10:42,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes.

164
00:10:42,364 --> 00:10:43,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that it

165
00:10:46,434 --> 00:10:47,995
[SPEAKER_00]: taught you how certain things worked.

166
00:10:49,395 --> 00:10:55,197
[SPEAKER_00]: By that, I mean just the old vacuum pump or the picture pump, how it worked.

167
00:10:55,237 --> 00:10:58,538
[SPEAKER_00]: We had to draw drawings and everything, but it was interesting to learn how those things worked.

168
00:10:58,578 --> 00:11:13,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think a lot of that is carried over because right now, I don't fool around with color television so much because that's a little more complicated, but with the black and white sets, we had very seldom did I have a service call.

169
00:11:13,102 --> 00:11:14,703
[SPEAKER_00]: I just went and taped the tubes

170
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:19,231
[SPEAKER_00]: and check them and see what, tap a few in here and there.

171
00:11:19,271 --> 00:11:20,691
[SPEAKER_00]: You live nearby.

172
00:11:20,731 --> 00:11:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

173
00:11:23,191 --> 00:11:27,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, after graduation then, what happened that summer of 28?

174
00:11:27,332 --> 00:11:30,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the summer of 28, I played in a local team.

175
00:11:30,673 --> 00:11:31,933
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I was just 16.

176
00:11:31,973 --> 00:11:42,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I played with the adult team over in Hainetown when I was 15 in 1927 called the Akron Worcester Coach Lines team.

177
00:11:42,134 --> 00:11:45,115
[SPEAKER_00]: We played around Akron and as far as Millersburg.

178
00:11:46,336 --> 00:11:50,638
[SPEAKER_00]: The next year, the same team, same group, different uniform, called the Jackrabbit Lions.

179
00:11:50,658 --> 00:11:53,078
[SPEAKER_00]: The same owner, everything else, but just a different team.

180
00:11:53,959 --> 00:11:57,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they kind of- Did they play very extensive schedules?

181
00:11:57,840 --> 00:11:59,440
[SPEAKER_00]: We played every Saturday and Sunday.

182
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:00,981
[SPEAKER_00]: About 40 games a week?

183
00:12:01,361 --> 00:12:03,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I don't be able to monitor that much, really.

184
00:12:03,902 --> 00:12:06,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Once in a while, but maybe in the evening, during the week, but very, very seldom.

185
00:12:06,943 --> 00:12:08,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Mostly holidays, Saturdays and Sundays.

186
00:12:08,863 --> 00:12:09,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're pitching?

187
00:12:09,863 --> 00:12:10,404
[SPEAKER_00]: I was pitching.

188
00:12:10,444 --> 00:12:11,944
[SPEAKER_00]: I pitched most all the games, yes.

189
00:12:13,004 --> 00:12:13,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And I played-

190
00:12:15,143 --> 00:12:20,324
[SPEAKER_00]: with our team, and then in 28, and then in 29, see, I hadn't signed up until I was 18.

191
00:12:20,344 --> 00:12:21,504
[SPEAKER_00]: When did you do that next?

192
00:12:22,005 --> 00:12:25,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Next year, I worked over at the Howard Box Board, and that helped me.

193
00:12:25,685 --> 00:12:28,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't want to work in a factory all my life.

194
00:12:28,926 --> 00:12:32,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was kind of a tough work, because my hands were all scarred up from that.

195
00:12:33,567 --> 00:12:38,108
[SPEAKER_00]: The paper, the yellow matches, is the paper that matches are made out of.

196
00:12:39,129 --> 00:12:45,090
[SPEAKER_00]: And they use that silicate to paste several layers of it together, and those edges are so sharp when they cut them.

197
00:12:45,862 --> 00:12:49,725
[SPEAKER_00]: In big rolls, we cut them in rolls that weighed 200 pounds, I suppose.

198
00:12:50,385 --> 00:12:55,950
[SPEAKER_00]: About 15 inches wide and about four feet, oh, maybe four to five feet in diameter.

199
00:12:56,290 --> 00:13:02,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And my job as a finisher was to wrap those with another paper for shipment.

200
00:13:02,695 --> 00:13:08,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, and then pick up the, what they call broke, the scrap, and stuff it in another place.

201
00:13:08,079 --> 00:13:10,561
[SPEAKER_00]: And that scrap was too terribly sharp on the edges.

202
00:13:10,581 --> 00:13:13,363
[SPEAKER_00]: My hands were cut all the time, all chapped up.

203
00:13:14,710 --> 00:13:15,910
[SPEAKER_00]: How long did you work there?

204
00:13:16,291 --> 00:13:20,052
[SPEAKER_00]: I worked here almost a year to the day.

205
00:13:20,072 --> 00:13:28,315
[SPEAKER_00]: A week before I left, I turned in my notice the day I got a raise, my first raise, because I had signed in the meantime, and they knew I was going to work.

206
00:13:28,335 --> 00:13:29,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell me about your first signing.

207
00:13:29,975 --> 00:13:31,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Who scouted you?

208
00:13:31,236 --> 00:13:34,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Cleveland scout, a man named Bill Bradley.

209
00:13:34,417 --> 00:13:36,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, Bill Bradley.

210
00:13:36,757 --> 00:13:37,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Signed me.

211
00:13:37,698 --> 00:13:43,880
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know who all contacted him to have him come down and see me play at the reports here.

212
00:13:44,702 --> 00:13:46,963
[SPEAKER_00]: performance has been in papers, maybe?

213
00:13:47,624 --> 00:14:12,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably a little write-ups, but there was a fellow, there was a couple of fellows, the manager of the first team I played on, named Bill Wepley, was, he thought I ought to have a chance someplace, and then another guy named, what was his name, I had it on the tip of my tongue a minute ago, he lived in Akron, I never saw him very often, but he claimed that he was the one that got Bill to come down, Bill Bradley to come down and see me.

214
00:14:14,550 --> 00:14:16,991
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't, of course, know he was in the stands that day.

215
00:14:17,652 --> 00:14:24,255
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a serious accident that happened the day that he first saw me in the very first inning.

216
00:14:26,457 --> 00:14:29,278
[SPEAKER_00]: We were in Millersburg playing against the Millersburg team.

217
00:14:29,298 --> 00:14:31,059
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a left-handed hitter up there.

218
00:14:31,079 --> 00:14:36,882
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, at that time, I had a little harder time throwing the left-handers than I did the right-handers.

219
00:14:36,902 --> 00:14:39,003
[SPEAKER_00]: For some reason, I could throw them and keep away.

220
00:14:39,023 --> 00:14:44,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, I got the count to three and three balls and one strike.

221
00:14:44,807 --> 00:14:46,588
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was rearing back and throwing.

222
00:14:46,709 --> 00:14:49,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And for some reason or other, this hitter didn't see the ball at all.

223
00:14:49,551 --> 00:14:50,451
[SPEAKER_00]: He never made a move.

224
00:14:50,612 --> 00:14:51,692
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit him right in the temple.

225
00:14:52,873 --> 00:14:54,495
[SPEAKER_00]: And he went down like an ox.

226
00:14:55,595 --> 00:14:57,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And they made him lay there a little while.

227
00:14:57,817 --> 00:15:01,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they let him, after a few minutes, he came to.

228
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:04,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And they let him come up, get up, and go over the bench.

229
00:15:04,763 --> 00:15:06,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And he got over as far as the bench.

230
00:15:06,084 --> 00:15:07,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And he fell over again, out.

231
00:15:09,326 --> 00:15:11,408
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, that shook me up pretty bad.

232
00:15:12,245 --> 00:15:13,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And they took him away to the hospital.

233
00:15:13,726 --> 00:15:15,267
[SPEAKER_00]: He was in the hospital for a while.

234
00:15:16,208 --> 00:15:27,515
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think indirectly it may have been the cause of his death, maybe 20 some years later, because he did die prematurely of something that may have affected him.

235
00:15:27,535 --> 00:15:28,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he was in the heart.

236
00:15:28,755 --> 00:15:29,676
[SPEAKER_00]: I could feel pretty hard.

237
00:15:30,777 --> 00:15:33,759
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, that shook me up.

238
00:15:33,839 --> 00:15:35,880
[SPEAKER_00]: I lasted a couple more days, but that was a while.

239
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:37,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they took me out.

240
00:15:37,401 --> 00:15:40,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But I guess I threw hard enough that he came back to see me later.

241
00:15:41,882 --> 00:15:45,363
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I got a chance to go out and play.

242
00:15:45,583 --> 00:15:48,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And that fall, he came down and signed me for the following year.

243
00:15:49,145 --> 00:15:50,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Very substantial amount.

244
00:15:51,306 --> 00:15:51,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

245
00:15:52,026 --> 00:15:53,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I got a chance to play.

246
00:15:54,267 --> 00:15:54,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

247
00:15:54,767 --> 00:15:56,067
[SPEAKER_00]: No bonus.

248
00:15:56,248 --> 00:15:59,309
[SPEAKER_00]: There hadn't been any bonuses given out at that time, to my knowledge.

249
00:16:00,269 --> 00:16:03,230
[SPEAKER_00]: I got $175 a month, which was pretty high.

250
00:16:03,890 --> 00:16:04,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Bradley signed you.

251
00:16:04,891 --> 00:16:06,872
[SPEAKER_00]: This is now the end of the 29.

252
00:16:07,092 --> 00:16:08,692
[SPEAKER_00]: End of 29 season.

253
00:16:09,032 --> 00:16:10,513
[SPEAKER_00]: For Cleveland.

254
00:16:10,910 --> 00:16:11,650
[SPEAKER_00]: affiliate team.

255
00:16:11,751 --> 00:16:19,855
[SPEAKER_00]: No, and I didn't know that was wrong, but it was illegal baseball-wise for him to sign me to a Johnstown, Pennsylvania contract.

256
00:16:19,875 --> 00:16:23,717
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a friend that was managing there, and he signed me for him.

257
00:16:25,598 --> 00:16:30,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And he actually signed me, but he signed me as a representative of Cleveland to a Johnstown contract.

258
00:16:30,381 --> 00:16:32,682
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well, don't worry about that, you know.

259
00:16:32,863 --> 00:16:33,823
[SPEAKER_00]: What was the point of that?

260
00:16:34,784 --> 00:16:36,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Couldn't Cleveland sign you directly?

261
00:16:36,425 --> 00:16:37,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they could have.

262
00:16:37,305 --> 00:16:40,487
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess they didn't have any room or whether you were old enough to sign.

263
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:43,341
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to have my parents' signatures.

264
00:16:43,361 --> 00:16:44,981
[SPEAKER_00]: You did, huh?

265
00:16:45,001 --> 00:16:45,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

266
00:16:45,802 --> 00:16:47,522
[SPEAKER_00]: You were still under 18, then?

267
00:16:47,562 --> 00:16:52,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, we had to be 21 to sign, yeah.

268
00:16:52,724 --> 00:16:54,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was 17 when I signed.

269
00:16:55,245 --> 00:16:56,366
[SPEAKER_00]: What did your parents think of this?

270
00:16:57,406 --> 00:16:59,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they were all for it if I wanted to do it.

271
00:16:59,207 --> 00:16:59,947
[SPEAKER_00]: They were all in it.

272
00:17:00,487 --> 00:17:01,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Were they pleased?

273
00:17:03,549 --> 00:17:04,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they never seemed otherwise.

274
00:17:04,829 --> 00:17:06,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, later on they were pleased.

275
00:17:06,190 --> 00:17:09,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, after things started going fairly well for me, but...

276
00:17:10,908 --> 00:17:13,710
[SPEAKER_00]: They never stood in the way or anything like that.

277
00:17:14,270 --> 00:17:20,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Never advised me against it, but that's what you want to do when you get a chance to do it.

278
00:17:20,575 --> 00:17:33,303
[SPEAKER_00]: My other brother, the middle one, one just older than I am, four years, he worked on a farm there and that was almost enough help for the farm, 150 acres what we had, so he worked there.

279
00:17:33,323 --> 00:17:38,066
[SPEAKER_00]: He really didn't leave me on the farm because even the year before I worked in the factory.

280
00:17:38,947 --> 00:17:40,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you do it that winter?

281
00:17:41,576 --> 00:17:43,376
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the depth of the depression.

282
00:17:43,396 --> 00:17:51,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Jobs were pretty hard to come by, and all I did was there was a friend that was interested in baseball that had a service station.

283
00:17:51,458 --> 00:17:54,319
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd work for him a couple hours a week, something like that, once in a while.

284
00:17:54,339 --> 00:17:58,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of the fall of the crash, I guess, wasn't it?

285
00:17:58,560 --> 00:17:59,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, right.

286
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:08,602
[SPEAKER_00]: I went back, and out of my $175 a month for the four months, I came back with $400 and some dollars, I'd say, and I lost it in the bank.

287
00:18:11,664 --> 00:18:13,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Who played the 29th seed then?

288
00:18:13,725 --> 00:18:16,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this was 30.

289
00:18:16,347 --> 00:18:18,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, 30 was on our bank down here.

290
00:18:18,208 --> 00:18:19,549
[SPEAKER_00]: The old bank down here went under.

291
00:18:19,989 --> 00:18:20,289
[SPEAKER_00]: All right.

292
00:18:20,329 --> 00:18:22,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, did you go to spring training in 30?

293
00:18:22,691 --> 00:18:24,252
[SPEAKER_00]: I went to spring training with 30.

294
00:18:25,072 --> 00:18:25,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Johnstown?

295
00:18:25,793 --> 00:18:27,133
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't go away to train.

296
00:18:27,153 --> 00:18:29,195
[SPEAKER_00]: They went right in their park, yeah.

297
00:18:29,995 --> 00:18:32,377
[SPEAKER_00]: We trained about 10 days before the season started.

298
00:18:32,397 --> 00:18:35,138
[SPEAKER_00]: I reported April 4th, if my memory serves me right.

299
00:18:35,318 --> 00:18:36,919
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite a memory.

300
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:39,421
[SPEAKER_00]: April 4th, and I think about April 12th,

301
00:18:40,098 --> 00:18:40,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see, 4-10.

302
00:18:41,098 --> 00:18:42,338
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it was about the 15th.

303
00:18:42,858 --> 00:18:47,399
[SPEAKER_00]: About 10 days, 10, 11 days later, the season opened.

304
00:18:47,639 --> 00:18:50,460
[SPEAKER_00]: And who was the manager there?

305
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:59,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Wilbur Good was his name, a left-handed hitting and throwing outfielder that played with the, I think it was the Braves.

306
00:19:00,021 --> 00:19:01,402
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was the Braves.

307
00:19:02,042 --> 00:19:04,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And he and Bill Bragg, we were pretty good friends, and so.

308
00:19:05,702 --> 00:19:08,103
[SPEAKER_00]: He was just an independently owned manager.

309
00:19:08,123 --> 00:19:08,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

310
00:19:10,733 --> 00:19:12,314
[SPEAKER_00]: What kind of a ballpark do they have there?

311
00:19:13,094 --> 00:19:14,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Same one they have now, Point Stadium.

312
00:19:14,775 --> 00:19:21,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about 250 or 60 feet to left field, if it's that far.

313
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:26,482
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a little over the width of a football field, to left field, with a high screen.

314
00:19:26,682 --> 00:19:28,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, right field is the length of a football field.

315
00:19:29,044 --> 00:19:30,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It's 400 and something feet.

316
00:19:30,425 --> 00:19:31,485
[SPEAKER_00]: It's as long as a football field.

317
00:19:32,226 --> 00:19:32,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

318
00:19:34,327 --> 00:19:37,989
[SPEAKER_00]: They still have a pretty large amateur tournament there every year now.

319
00:19:38,009 --> 00:19:39,550
[SPEAKER_00]: They have a professional team that does that.

320
00:19:40,257 --> 00:19:41,238
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't now anymore.

321
00:19:41,338 --> 00:19:45,940
[SPEAKER_00]: They had quite a while after I was there, yes, sir.

322
00:19:46,421 --> 00:19:49,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Up until August 10 years ago.

323
00:19:49,302 --> 00:19:51,584
[SPEAKER_00]: I can see my notes here.

324
00:19:52,504 --> 00:19:56,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I have to... Do I upset you?

325
00:19:56,747 --> 00:19:57,327
[SPEAKER_00]: No, hold on.

326
00:19:57,367 --> 00:19:58,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's see.

327
00:19:58,468 --> 00:20:02,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think I missed the one on the plug.

328
00:20:08,742 --> 00:20:13,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Who were some of the teams in that league that year?

329
00:20:13,205 --> 00:20:14,386
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

330
00:20:16,167 --> 00:20:31,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Parksburg and Fairmont, North Virginia, Wheeling, Charlotte, Pennsylvania, Johnstown.

331
00:20:32,218 --> 00:20:33,078
[SPEAKER_00]: How did you travel?

332
00:20:34,499 --> 00:20:35,340
[SPEAKER_00]: We had three

333
00:20:38,001 --> 00:20:40,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Big old Hudson Super Six.

334
00:20:41,142 --> 00:20:45,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Buy us seven passenger sedans.

335
00:20:45,763 --> 00:20:49,023
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know where they got them.

336
00:20:49,063 --> 00:20:53,404
[SPEAKER_00]: They were owned by the club, and one of the players would drive each one.

337
00:20:53,444 --> 00:20:54,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way it was done.

338
00:20:54,684 --> 00:20:56,805
[SPEAKER_00]: There were a lot of ways to keep track of them.

339
00:20:57,365 --> 00:21:07,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, actually, sometimes there was not enough room in those, so a couple of guys had cars that they gave them a few cents a mile to drive their own cars.

340
00:21:08,062 --> 00:21:09,663
[SPEAKER_00]: and take somebody with them.

341
00:21:10,284 --> 00:21:11,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's right.

342
00:21:12,786 --> 00:21:15,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you... probably overnight trips?

343
00:21:16,069 --> 00:21:19,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yes, we stayed... You had to have one of those places.

344
00:21:19,552 --> 00:21:22,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the roads being what they were, you couldn't come back at night.

345
00:21:22,854 --> 00:21:24,416
[SPEAKER_00]: It would take too long.

346
00:21:24,676 --> 00:21:27,218
[SPEAKER_00]: We went to parks, but we stayed in hotels every place.

347
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:31,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Huntington was in one year, part of the year.

348
00:21:31,762 --> 00:21:33,503
[SPEAKER_00]: That'd be quite a trip.

349
00:21:33,543 --> 00:21:36,686
[SPEAKER_00]: How was your travel allowance?

350
00:21:38,143 --> 00:21:44,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, of course, they paid for the traveling, and we got $2 a day, meal money, when we were on the road, yeah.

351
00:21:50,092 --> 00:21:52,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Any night baseball on the road?

352
00:21:52,294 --> 00:21:54,655
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the year it started, 1930.

353
00:21:56,337 --> 00:21:57,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But we only played a couple nights a week.

354
00:21:58,178 --> 00:22:00,660
[SPEAKER_00]: We, at Johnstown... Did they have lights there?

355
00:22:02,666 --> 00:22:03,446
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you got me.

356
00:22:03,486 --> 00:22:04,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether they had them the first year.

357
00:22:04,767 --> 00:22:07,367
[SPEAKER_00]: I know they had, we played some in the second year.

358
00:22:07,407 --> 00:22:10,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think we played all day in the first year there.

359
00:22:10,368 --> 00:22:15,069
[SPEAKER_00]: But I believe one other team, I believe maybe Wheeling had lights in 1930.

360
00:22:15,209 --> 00:22:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, the minor leagues had lights before Cincinnati, and the bigger leagues had it, the smaller minors had it.

361
00:22:22,891 --> 00:22:25,792
[SPEAKER_00]: There weren't much lights, though, were there?

362
00:22:25,992 --> 00:22:26,932
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

363
00:22:27,713 --> 00:22:28,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it help pitches?

364
00:22:31,534 --> 00:22:37,899
[SPEAKER_00]: It helped pitchers until the ball was hit, and then they kind of reversed the process because the fielders couldn't see the ball.

365
00:22:38,160 --> 00:22:39,601
[SPEAKER_00]: See that?

366
00:22:40,381 --> 00:22:44,285
[SPEAKER_00]: If the batter hit it, then they evened back again, I think.

367
00:22:44,625 --> 00:22:45,946
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way I always thought about it.

368
00:22:46,967 --> 00:22:50,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And contrary to what a lot of people thought, the fastball would be a great pitch at night.

369
00:22:51,170 --> 00:22:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a breaking pitch because they couldn't see the rotation.

370
00:22:57,473 --> 00:23:00,955
[SPEAKER_00]: as effective or more so than a fastball in life, especially in the higher leagues.

371
00:23:02,776 --> 00:23:05,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems, though, the better hitters follow the rotation.

372
00:23:05,778 --> 00:23:12,181
[SPEAKER_00]: The earlier they can pick it up, the better chance they have of hitting the ball or taking it or whatever.

373
00:23:13,262 --> 00:23:17,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they couldn't see it until it was closer to the bend, they had less time to adjust.

374
00:23:17,584 --> 00:23:21,126
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my theory, at least.

375
00:23:21,226 --> 00:23:26,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think I've had some pretty good hitters tell me that the breaking pitch was more effective.

376
00:23:27,645 --> 00:23:37,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Were there any players in the Middle Atlantic League that first year and the second year you were there that later achieved great fame?

377
00:23:37,589 --> 00:23:49,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first year that I was there, there were, I can remember one of the old umpires saying there's just two guys in the league that he had thought had a chance of going to the big leagues.

378
00:23:49,974 --> 00:23:52,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And one, I was one of them, and Harry Gumber.

379
00:23:53,302 --> 00:23:54,983
[SPEAKER_00]: was the other one, and then we both did.

380
00:23:55,023 --> 00:24:03,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, however, we had an older fellow on our team that made it for a little while named Harvey Thompson, the pitcher.

381
00:24:03,908 --> 00:24:04,989
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't hang around long.

382
00:24:05,009 --> 00:24:08,011
[SPEAKER_00]: He wasn't around probably in the big leagues over part of a year or so.

383
00:24:08,031 --> 00:24:08,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's about all I know.

384
00:24:09,152 --> 00:24:10,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, was this the Classic C League?

385
00:24:11,113 --> 00:24:12,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That was C League at that time.

386
00:24:13,714 --> 00:24:19,538
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have expected a few more, wouldn't you, with the C classification that might look good here?

387
00:24:19,978 --> 00:24:22,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it was a lot more competitive at that time.

388
00:24:23,942 --> 00:24:27,303
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, that was considered to be a pretty good C League.

389
00:24:28,663 --> 00:24:32,644
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, every league thinks they're better than another league, the same classification.

390
00:24:32,884 --> 00:24:38,366
[SPEAKER_00]: They used to say their C League, the players would say that's better than New York Penn League, which was a B League at that time.

391
00:24:38,386 --> 00:24:39,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't know.

392
00:24:39,446 --> 00:24:42,007
[SPEAKER_00]: I never saw any New York Penn League play at that time.

393
00:24:42,927 --> 00:24:47,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Were the ballparks generally pretty nice parks?

394
00:24:47,789 --> 00:24:49,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they varied just as they do now.

395
00:24:49,789 --> 00:24:53,010
[SPEAKER_00]: There were some fairly decent ones and then some real bad ones.

396
00:24:53,550 --> 00:24:58,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Charleroi had a bad park, just bad old wooden fence and wooden stands.

397
00:24:58,312 --> 00:25:00,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It was poor lights.

398
00:25:01,393 --> 00:25:03,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was a river just outside right field.

399
00:25:04,013 --> 00:25:05,594
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of balls in batting practice.

400
00:25:05,614 --> 00:25:06,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to be careful.

401
00:25:06,534 --> 00:25:09,575
[SPEAKER_00]: The balls would be gone before batting practice was over.

402
00:25:10,295 --> 00:25:14,917
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, because the tree would be gone short.

403
00:25:16,137 --> 00:25:17,057
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the year.

404
00:25:19,098 --> 00:25:20,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Scottsdale was another team in the league.

405
00:25:20,759 --> 00:25:22,079
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reason I say that.

406
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:28,202
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the year it played against a guy named Mickey King, who played at Scottsdale in the Cardinal chain.

407
00:25:28,482 --> 00:25:30,042
[SPEAKER_00]: It was Joe Medwick who played it.

408
00:25:30,963 --> 00:25:31,883
[SPEAKER_00]: He was still in school.

409
00:25:32,383 --> 00:25:39,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was playing there under a different name as a pro during the summer until he finished his school, high school, I guess it was.

410
00:25:40,105 --> 00:25:41,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So he had his own ability.

411
00:25:41,406 --> 00:25:41,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

412
00:25:42,726 --> 00:25:43,586
[SPEAKER_00]: And he came back.

413
00:25:43,626 --> 00:25:47,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And afterwards, they talked about Medwick and he played at Scottsdale.

414
00:25:47,228 --> 00:25:48,748
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said, I don't remember any Medwick.

415
00:25:49,778 --> 00:25:50,939
[SPEAKER_00]: You remember Mickey King?

416
00:25:51,439 --> 00:25:52,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I remember him.

417
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:55,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Was he pretty tough?

418
00:25:55,602 --> 00:25:58,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was a wild slinger just like he was all the rest of his career.

419
00:25:59,164 --> 00:26:05,548
[SPEAKER_00]: He hit a ball out of the park up here, down here, just as well or more so than if it was right in the middle of the play.

420
00:26:07,309 --> 00:26:08,350
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a bad ball hitter.

421
00:26:14,237 --> 00:26:15,578
[SPEAKER_00]: of a career, good career.

422
00:26:15,838 --> 00:26:18,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Any very unusual things happen in those years at Johnston?

423
00:26:18,679 --> 00:26:21,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it crazy plays or exciting plays?

424
00:26:21,420 --> 00:26:22,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything stand out in your mind?

425
00:26:22,841 --> 00:26:27,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing that I remember too much as being exceptionally funny.

426
00:26:28,304 --> 00:26:32,806
[SPEAKER_00]: One exceptionally bad experience that I had was in a playoff in 1931.

427
00:26:33,706 --> 00:26:41,310
[SPEAKER_00]: I know to this day, of course, the fellow, he was old then, so he's bound not to be living in his umpire in the game.

428
00:26:42,628 --> 00:26:45,189
[SPEAKER_00]: And we're the visiting club in Charleston, West Virginia.

429
00:26:46,570 --> 00:26:48,471
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the final game of the playoffs.

430
00:26:48,491 --> 00:26:49,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it in Charleston?

431
00:26:49,472 --> 00:26:50,132
[SPEAKER_00]: In Charleston.

432
00:26:50,752 --> 00:26:51,933
[SPEAKER_00]: We were the visiting team.

433
00:26:53,054 --> 00:26:54,594
[SPEAKER_00]: And it got into the 11th inning.

434
00:26:55,275 --> 00:26:56,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It was 2 and 2.

435
00:26:57,396 --> 00:27:02,799
[SPEAKER_00]: And I never had so many balls called in my life on balls that were over the plate.

436
00:27:02,859 --> 00:27:05,620
[SPEAKER_00]: It got down to where the bases were loaded and two out.

437
00:27:06,361 --> 00:27:07,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And this guy, at bat,

438
00:27:09,656 --> 00:27:13,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And I threw the first three pitches right down the middle, because I knew I had an idea.

439
00:27:14,179 --> 00:27:15,340
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was ball three.

440
00:27:17,061 --> 00:27:19,143
[SPEAKER_00]: He walked the guy, and the manager was so mad.

441
00:27:19,303 --> 00:27:21,685
[SPEAKER_00]: We were all complaining, of course.

442
00:27:21,705 --> 00:27:22,746
[SPEAKER_00]: We beat us.

443
00:27:22,786 --> 00:27:24,327
[SPEAKER_00]: He walked the guy in to end the game.

444
00:27:24,347 --> 00:27:25,027
[SPEAKER_00]: He wanted to go home.

445
00:27:26,148 --> 00:27:27,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the playoff final of the playoff game.

446
00:27:28,850 --> 00:27:32,232
[SPEAKER_00]: The guy's name was Clark.

447
00:27:34,754 --> 00:27:36,435
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't want to umpire any longer.

448
00:27:36,476 --> 00:27:37,076
[SPEAKER_00]: He was tired.

449
00:27:38,192 --> 00:27:39,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he wanted to go home.

450
00:27:39,993 --> 00:27:43,916
[SPEAKER_00]: That's one way he could end it by making it the last half of the inning.

451
00:27:43,936 --> 00:27:45,777
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd walk that run in and the game's over.

452
00:27:45,877 --> 00:27:48,379
[SPEAKER_00]: That conspicuously?

453
00:27:48,659 --> 00:27:49,040
Brutal.

454
00:27:49,340 --> 00:27:49,960
[SPEAKER_00]: It was brutal.

455
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:50,861
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll tell you that.

456
00:27:50,941 --> 00:27:55,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in hometown there was no other fans from our place.

457
00:27:56,565 --> 00:27:57,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing, just our airs.

458
00:27:58,866 --> 00:28:01,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Called it a squawk, but it was the worst thing you ever saw.

459
00:28:03,450 --> 00:28:06,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Excepting one thing, out in the Coast League when I was all through, coming back down there,

460
00:28:08,038 --> 00:28:08,979
[SPEAKER_00]: I was with Seattle.

461
00:28:09,979 --> 00:28:11,780
[SPEAKER_00]: When was this again?

462
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:12,120
[SPEAKER_00]: 1950.

463
00:28:13,321 --> 00:28:20,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And then a guy named Al Summers was umpiring back in the parade, and our catcher was Mickey Grasso, who had a habit of baiting umpires.

464
00:28:20,925 --> 00:28:25,148
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's no reason for the umpire to take it out in calling pitches.

465
00:28:25,808 --> 00:28:27,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And he cost us the playoff.

466
00:28:27,169 --> 00:28:37,395
[SPEAKER_00]: We were playing San Diego in San Diego for a one-game playoff to determine, let's see,

467
00:28:38,044 --> 00:28:41,326
[SPEAKER_00]: to determine which one of us got into the Shaughnessy system playoffs.

468
00:28:41,386 --> 00:28:41,907
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess it was.

469
00:28:42,607 --> 00:28:47,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's in the bottom half of... We got an inning to go.

470
00:28:47,811 --> 00:28:51,113
[SPEAKER_00]: We get one bat yet, and they get two bats, this one and the next one.

471
00:28:51,693 --> 00:28:55,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And he started calling balls right down the middle, and his grandson was jumping up and down.

472
00:28:56,096 --> 00:29:00,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the only time I ever, ever honestly pressed a guy.

473
00:29:00,859 --> 00:29:06,523
[SPEAKER_00]: I walked in, I told him what I thought of him, the umpire, and he didn't kick me out either, because he knew he was wrong.

474
00:29:07,913 --> 00:29:15,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And later on, I talked to an umpire in Akron, went down to one, he runs an umpire school, if you can imagine that, in Florida now.

475
00:29:16,081 --> 00:29:29,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And he talked about, this guy in Akron said, he talked about it, he said the only time he ever didn't throw somebody out was, and he mentioned me, he said he was wrong, he admitted to them that he was wrong in doing that.

476
00:29:29,993 --> 00:29:31,975
[SPEAKER_00]: That didn't help us as far as, see?

477
00:29:34,625 --> 00:29:35,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are funny things.

478
00:29:36,446 --> 00:29:38,487
[SPEAKER_00]: I know I've seen other...

479
00:29:38,627 --> 00:29:42,909
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen guys at various stages now in big leagues.

480
00:29:42,969 --> 00:29:50,092
[SPEAKER_00]: I saw a guy in a rundown, his cap come off, and as he's running back, he picked up the cap while he was in a chase.

481
00:29:50,912 --> 00:29:51,833
[SPEAKER_00]: You can imagine that.

482
00:29:52,113 --> 00:29:54,534
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a reflex, I guess.

483
00:29:54,634 --> 00:29:56,115
[SPEAKER_00]: He's going back the other way.

484
00:29:56,135 --> 00:29:57,015
[SPEAKER_00]: He stopped and picked it up.

485
00:29:57,035 --> 00:30:01,037
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't stop, but stooped and picked up his cap, just reached down and got it while he was running back.

486
00:30:04,513 --> 00:30:17,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of hard to think of all the funny things on the spur of the moment type thing, things that maybe seemed very funny then, knowing the characters involved, to try to tell it and have it sound funny.

487
00:30:17,482 --> 00:30:19,404
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not near as funny now.

488
00:30:20,544 --> 00:30:28,430
[SPEAKER_00]: But I often wish and regretted that I didn't keep a diary, so to speak, of things that went on.

489
00:30:28,790 --> 00:30:31,572
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have made quite some funny things.

490
00:30:34,528 --> 00:30:43,893
[SPEAKER_00]: OK, you're Johnstown at 31, Fort Wayne at 32.

491
00:30:43,913 --> 00:30:46,315
[SPEAKER_00]: What happened here now to this state?

492
00:30:46,515 --> 00:30:58,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, at the end of, not at the end, but along about the middle of the 31 season, I was still property of Johnstown on paper.

493
00:30:58,822 --> 00:31:00,563
[SPEAKER_00]: I started pitching pretty good.

494
00:31:00,583 --> 00:31:01,804
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a good earning run average.

495
00:31:01,824 --> 00:31:03,345
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't win but 11 ball games.

496
00:31:03,766 --> 00:31:14,910
[SPEAKER_00]: in that short season, but I had a lot of ballgames that could have gone either way, that maybe a one-run loss or maybe an error, an out-run or something like that, but I had a pretty good...

497
00:31:14,930 --> 00:31:18,692
[SPEAKER_00]: So they purchased me on paper, it was announced that I was purchased by Cuban.

498
00:31:20,072 --> 00:31:27,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Then to get to 32 then, I went to spring training in 1932 with the Indians in New Orleans, the first big league spring training.

499
00:31:27,996 --> 00:31:31,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, they had no intention of keeping me at that time, that was just a...

500
00:31:32,687 --> 00:31:36,349
[SPEAKER_00]: to be down there and work out and get a taste of it, I suppose.

501
00:31:37,550 --> 00:31:41,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So along about, and I stayed on the club.

502
00:31:41,712 --> 00:31:45,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Back up north here, I wasn't officially on the playing roster, I don't think.

503
00:31:46,235 --> 00:31:50,738
[SPEAKER_00]: But I was with them and all sat on the bench and everything for the first couple weeks of the season.

504
00:31:50,758 --> 00:31:57,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Then they opted me out to Fort Wayne because they were waiting to have a place to put me, I guess.

505
00:31:59,123 --> 00:32:01,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, Bill Wombly was the manager over there.

506
00:32:02,133 --> 00:32:03,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Triple play guy.

507
00:32:03,453 --> 00:32:06,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Elmer Smith, the home run, the base loaded guy, played outfield for a while.

508
00:32:06,934 --> 00:32:07,755
[SPEAKER_00]: I interviewed Elmer Smith.

509
00:32:08,215 --> 00:32:08,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you?

510
00:32:09,355 --> 00:32:11,496
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't get the one, but he's healthy and good.

511
00:32:11,536 --> 00:32:12,376
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't want to talk.

512
00:32:12,836 --> 00:32:13,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is that right?

513
00:32:14,076 --> 00:32:15,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I didn't know that.

514
00:32:15,857 --> 00:32:16,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry to hear that.

515
00:32:17,137 --> 00:32:20,318
[SPEAKER_00]: I used to see him quite often when I was scouting up in Cleveland around Lakewood there.

516
00:32:20,338 --> 00:32:21,518
[SPEAKER_00]: He was out there, and I'd see him.

517
00:32:21,538 --> 00:32:22,358
[SPEAKER_00]: I grew up in Lakewood.

518
00:32:22,659 --> 00:32:23,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, did you?

519
00:32:23,979 --> 00:32:24,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

520
00:32:24,619 --> 00:32:26,780
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually interviewed Bill Loppy in 1935 when I was in

521
00:32:28,222 --> 00:32:29,043
[SPEAKER_00]: junior high school.

522
00:32:29,123 --> 00:32:29,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, did you?

523
00:32:29,643 --> 00:32:32,665
[SPEAKER_00]: I was a school reporter, but I didn't know what I was doing.

524
00:32:33,406 --> 00:32:37,309
[SPEAKER_00]: I would love to get up to him now, but he doesn't seem to want to tell him.

525
00:32:38,330 --> 00:32:40,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was managing Fort Wayne.

526
00:32:40,271 --> 00:32:41,212
[SPEAKER_00]: He managed Fort Wayne, yeah.

527
00:32:41,232 --> 00:32:43,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Just at Central Lake.

528
00:32:43,053 --> 00:32:44,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Central Lake was Class B.

529
00:32:44,594 --> 00:32:45,435
[SPEAKER_00]: At that time, yeah.

530
00:32:45,495 --> 00:32:46,756
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a real good year there.

531
00:32:47,356 --> 00:32:47,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes?

532
00:32:48,097 --> 00:32:48,457
[SPEAKER_00]: I did.

533
00:32:48,617 --> 00:32:51,339
[SPEAKER_00]: I had, I'd say, 14 and 6 there.

534
00:32:51,720 --> 00:32:52,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah?

535
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:55,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I played, uh, what are these?

536
00:32:55,702 --> 00:32:57,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Body conditions, living conditions.

537
00:32:58,142 --> 00:32:59,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Noticeably different from, yes.

538
00:33:00,984 --> 00:33:01,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Not so much.

539
00:33:01,564 --> 00:33:03,526
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't think so, as far as I would.

540
00:33:03,586 --> 00:33:04,586
[SPEAKER_00]: The town's the same.

541
00:33:04,606 --> 00:33:07,628
[SPEAKER_00]: The same depression and just a different year is all.

542
00:33:08,649 --> 00:33:09,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And travel facilities?

543
00:33:09,810 --> 00:33:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Travel.

544
00:33:10,410 --> 00:33:13,372
[SPEAKER_00]: We traveled in cars like that the same way.

545
00:33:14,113 --> 00:33:15,254
[SPEAKER_00]: We never was on a bus.

546
00:33:15,334 --> 00:33:22,178
[SPEAKER_00]: It was always there, individual cars, a group of cars.

547
00:33:22,198 --> 00:33:24,120
[SPEAKER_00]: See, South Bend was in there a little while.

548
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:27,682
[SPEAKER_00]: And here in Pennsylvania, Dayton, Fort Wayne,

549
00:33:32,964 --> 00:33:34,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Youngstown, Youngstown and Akron.

550
00:33:34,825 --> 00:33:36,366
[SPEAKER_00]: They were in the Central Division at the time.

551
00:33:36,586 --> 00:33:38,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you say you played in Akron?

552
00:33:38,547 --> 00:33:40,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you come home here at all?

553
00:33:40,308 --> 00:33:41,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, oh sure.

554
00:33:41,909 --> 00:33:44,210
[SPEAKER_00]: They allowed me to come home when I was in Akron.

555
00:33:46,411 --> 00:33:55,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Stayed home at night because we had about a three-game series, three days, a couple nights and after the last game we'd be on our way going someplace to the next play.

556
00:33:55,796 --> 00:33:56,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you have a car?

557
00:33:57,646 --> 00:33:58,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I had a car the first year.

558
00:33:59,047 --> 00:34:00,808
[SPEAKER_00]: I took my car to Spring Canyon the first year.

559
00:34:00,829 --> 00:34:02,510
[SPEAKER_00]: I bought it when I worked there one year.

560
00:34:02,530 --> 00:34:03,591
[SPEAKER_00]: I bought it from my brother.

561
00:34:03,611 --> 00:34:04,671
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought there was a depression.

562
00:34:05,492 --> 00:34:11,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll tell you the only reason I could buy it was I make $45 every two weeks.

563
00:34:11,937 --> 00:34:13,158
[SPEAKER_00]: My brother owned the car.

564
00:34:13,678 --> 00:34:15,200
[SPEAKER_00]: He had worked for my uncle for quite a bit.

565
00:34:15,220 --> 00:34:16,681
[SPEAKER_00]: My uncle bought it and gave it to him.

566
00:34:17,261 --> 00:34:18,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, they were only about $450.

567
00:34:18,402 --> 00:34:20,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Model A Roadster is what it was.

568
00:34:24,272 --> 00:34:28,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And the brother after, he didn't really care that much whether he had it or not.

569
00:34:28,074 --> 00:34:29,154
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, gee, I wanted to buy it.

570
00:34:29,174 --> 00:34:29,995
[SPEAKER_00]: So I bought it from him.

571
00:34:30,795 --> 00:34:34,517
[SPEAKER_00]: I gave him $10, $20, $25 every payday.

572
00:34:34,577 --> 00:34:35,778
[SPEAKER_00]: I paid it up that year, too.

573
00:34:36,678 --> 00:34:38,079
[SPEAKER_00]: You drove it all the way down to New Orleans?

574
00:34:38,639 --> 00:34:39,039
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

575
00:34:39,220 --> 00:34:41,040
[SPEAKER_00]: That was my first year, 1930.

576
00:34:41,080 --> 00:34:41,561
[SPEAKER_00]: I had it.

577
00:34:42,581 --> 00:34:44,002
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I didn't drive to New Orleans.

578
00:34:44,022 --> 00:34:45,343
[SPEAKER_00]: The car stayed home.

579
00:34:47,764 --> 00:34:50,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But I had it with me in 30.

580
00:34:50,825 --> 00:34:52,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And in 31, I didn't take it to John's town.

581
00:34:52,846 --> 00:34:53,987
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought, well, I'd better.

582
00:34:56,186 --> 00:35:04,233
[SPEAKER_00]: I took it once, and then when I come back to Akron, John Akson was in the Central League, or in the Mid-Atlantic at one time, too.

583
00:35:04,493 --> 00:35:11,419
[SPEAKER_00]: I come back to Akron, and I leave it home, and then go with the rest of the club.

584
00:35:11,439 --> 00:35:14,021
[SPEAKER_00]: They ticketed it one time while I was gone for a couple of weeks.

585
00:35:14,061 --> 00:35:17,184
[SPEAKER_00]: It was parked out by Point Stadium, and they towed it away.

586
00:35:17,204 --> 00:35:19,926
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought, well, I can't have that happen.

587
00:35:20,507 --> 00:35:22,088
[SPEAKER_00]: How would Bill Wong be as a manager?

588
00:35:23,824 --> 00:35:24,304
[SPEAKER_00]: I liked him.

589
00:35:24,324 --> 00:35:25,445
[SPEAKER_00]: He treated me fine.

590
00:35:25,705 --> 00:35:30,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, when a guy has a good year for somebody, they treat you pretty good.

591
00:35:30,287 --> 00:35:31,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have no complaints.

592
00:35:32,628 --> 00:35:35,810
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't recall too many other guys complaining about him.

593
00:35:35,890 --> 00:35:39,572
[SPEAKER_00]: It seemed like it might have been a little too easy going, possibly.

594
00:35:40,392 --> 00:35:42,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Were there any big stars on the team?

595
00:35:42,673 --> 00:35:43,173
[SPEAKER_00]: What rank?

596
00:35:44,214 --> 00:35:46,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Names that we heard from later?

597
00:35:47,575 --> 00:35:50,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Earl Bollinger.

598
00:35:51,749 --> 00:35:54,510
[SPEAKER_00]: If that name is familiar, he was on the team.

599
00:35:54,590 --> 00:35:55,471
[SPEAKER_00]: B-O-L-Y-A-R-D.

600
00:35:55,631 --> 00:36:00,493
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I've heard the name.

601
00:36:00,553 --> 00:36:01,273
[SPEAKER_00]: He was on that team.

602
00:36:01,333 --> 00:36:06,036
[SPEAKER_00]: And the rest of the guys were the younger guys didn't make it.

603
00:36:06,176 --> 00:36:17,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And some of the older guys had been maybe in bigger professional leagues, but had been working their way down to, so to speak, and just had a job in the summertime to make a living.

604
00:36:17,121 --> 00:36:18,141
[SPEAKER_00]: That's about what I remember.

605
00:36:18,161 --> 00:36:20,342
[SPEAKER_00]: There were some old fellas, some guys in their 30s playing

606
00:36:21,077 --> 00:36:25,399
[SPEAKER_00]: playing b-ball, man, just to make a living in the summertime.

607
00:36:28,181 --> 00:36:32,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, now how about the transfer to Orleans in 1933?

608
00:36:33,423 --> 00:36:44,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I went to, they called me in at their office, they recalled me from Fort Wayne, and Billy Evans, the general manager, called me in and said, now we're going to send you down to New Orleans outright.

609
00:36:45,049 --> 00:36:46,550
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another independent club, see?

610
00:36:48,621 --> 00:36:51,383
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't let that worry you because you still belong to us.

611
00:36:51,503 --> 00:36:59,087
[SPEAKER_00]: We just, we've got too many players under option and we can't send you there on option, so we're going to send you on right.

612
00:37:00,587 --> 00:37:01,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Which was another illegal thing.

613
00:37:01,988 --> 00:37:07,811
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not quite sure what that means legally, but... Well, it just was against the baseball rules.

614
00:37:08,572 --> 00:37:10,012
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know it, of course, at the time.

615
00:37:10,092 --> 00:37:12,413
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, well, sure, I wanted to play, that's all.

616
00:37:12,634 --> 00:37:13,174
[SPEAKER_00]: What the heck?

617
00:37:13,794 --> 00:37:15,335
[SPEAKER_00]: I went down there to spring training.

618
00:37:16,108 --> 00:37:18,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this a kind of a cover-up, you suppose?

619
00:37:18,789 --> 00:37:23,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it gave them a chance to have control of me.

620
00:37:23,491 --> 00:37:26,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of three or four years, about six, six or seven.

621
00:37:26,632 --> 00:37:28,053
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it amounted to.

622
00:37:28,073 --> 00:37:29,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So I went on down there.

623
00:37:29,434 --> 00:37:33,895
[SPEAKER_00]: And the first year, I didn't get started off very well.

624
00:37:33,996 --> 00:37:36,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, there are, again, I'm in passe ball.

625
00:37:36,296 --> 00:37:39,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And the passe was as good as the present triple, at least.

626
00:37:39,578 --> 00:37:44,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So I was considered a busher, a rookie.

627
00:37:45,417 --> 00:37:50,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And the class A men were doing the, they called A players and B players.

628
00:37:50,199 --> 00:37:55,161
[SPEAKER_00]: You had so many B players on the squad, but the rest could be A players.

629
00:37:56,061 --> 00:37:58,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So they had, or class men as they called them.

630
00:37:58,562 --> 00:38:04,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So the pitching staff consisted of four or five class men.

631
00:38:04,945 --> 00:38:11,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And as long as they were going in the earlier spring when the weather wasn't too bad, too hot, they were running along fairly good.

632
00:38:11,548 --> 00:38:12,568
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get to pitch much.

633
00:38:12,588 --> 00:38:13,368
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd leave once in a while.

634
00:38:13,389 --> 00:38:14,569
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about anything, of course.

635
00:38:14,925 --> 00:38:16,066
[SPEAKER_00]: I was one that needed work.

636
00:38:17,007 --> 00:38:21,450
[SPEAKER_00]: But then they started to falter when the weather got real hot.

637
00:38:22,350 --> 00:38:24,432
[SPEAKER_00]: I got in more, and finally I started winning.

638
00:38:24,512 --> 00:38:28,755
[SPEAKER_00]: And then once I got in regularly, they never got me back out again.

639
00:38:29,155 --> 00:38:39,182
[SPEAKER_00]: From July 2nd in 1933 through the playoffs, I won 21 games.

640
00:38:40,523 --> 00:38:43,365
[SPEAKER_00]: I won 17 in the season from that day on.

641
00:38:45,196 --> 00:38:50,759
[SPEAKER_00]: I won two in the league playoff and two in the Dixie Series.

642
00:38:50,779 --> 00:38:59,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Besides, during the stretch drive and during the playoffs, I was the relief man midway between my starts.

643
00:39:00,264 --> 00:39:02,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had, it so happened I relieved every time.

644
00:39:02,385 --> 00:39:10,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the second day, I'd pitch a pro game, rest a day, relieve the next day, rest a day, and then pitch my game.

645
00:39:10,910 --> 00:39:12,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And I did that through the Dixie Series, too.

646
00:39:14,652 --> 00:39:17,573
[SPEAKER_00]: And it happened almost the same way the following year.

647
00:39:18,333 --> 00:39:19,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And who was managing you?

648
00:39:20,173 --> 00:39:21,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Larry Gilbert.

649
00:39:21,453 --> 00:39:25,854
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the good, real fine managers.

650
00:39:25,954 --> 00:39:27,895
[SPEAKER_00]: One of my favorite managers, by the way.

651
00:39:28,695 --> 00:39:35,796
[SPEAKER_00]: He's deceased now, but he was the type ideal for a minor league manager.

652
00:39:36,196 --> 00:39:39,277
[SPEAKER_00]: And I suppose he had chances to manage in the big league.

653
00:39:40,053 --> 00:39:44,617
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't have controlled the players as well, because some of them, they get high and mighty in the big leagues.

654
00:39:45,057 --> 00:39:47,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Minor leaguers are trying to make it, and they listen.

655
00:39:47,639 --> 00:39:55,605
[SPEAKER_00]: But he would call a player or make a certain move, and he'd ask somebody on the bench, why did I do that?

656
00:39:55,625 --> 00:39:56,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you think I did that?

657
00:39:57,167 --> 00:39:59,468
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd explain his reason, and you learned it.

658
00:39:59,549 --> 00:40:01,210
[SPEAKER_00]: You learned baseball that way through him.

659
00:40:02,271 --> 00:40:07,575
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I got to say he was one of my favorite managers.

660
00:40:08,822 --> 00:40:11,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Just an example, it's a common example after you've been in the game.

661
00:40:12,423 --> 00:40:14,744
[SPEAKER_00]: The catcher would be the eighth hitter and the pitcher coming up.

662
00:40:15,004 --> 00:40:18,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe there'd be a man on second base.

663
00:40:18,565 --> 00:40:21,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a man on first base, I mean, and third, first and third.

664
00:40:22,426 --> 00:40:26,068
[SPEAKER_00]: And somebody said, well, why didn't he have the guy on first field?

665
00:40:26,868 --> 00:40:37,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd come around and say, well, would you rather have a man on first and third with a catcher hitting, or a man on first, second, and third with a lousy hitting pitcher now?

666
00:40:38,720 --> 00:40:39,680
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it amounted to.

667
00:40:39,700 --> 00:40:44,941
[SPEAKER_00]: The minute the guy stole a second, they'd walk the catcher and the pitcher would be hit.

668
00:40:45,041 --> 00:40:50,282
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, you got to keep that blocked up so you can get you a little better hitter hit.

669
00:40:52,363 --> 00:40:52,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And they do it.

670
00:40:52,863 --> 00:40:55,963
[SPEAKER_00]: I see that in the big leagues now.

671
00:40:55,983 --> 00:40:59,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that's right.

672
00:40:59,444 --> 00:41:00,404
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a different thing.

673
00:41:00,424 --> 00:41:05,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, of course, in the minor leagues, you had 17 players, and you had to let your pitcher stay in there as long as he could pitch.

674
00:41:07,385 --> 00:41:09,986
[SPEAKER_00]: was that you were limited in all the moves you could make.

675
00:41:10,146 --> 00:41:12,646
[SPEAKER_00]: The game is different now.

676
00:41:12,666 --> 00:41:18,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was on that New Orleans team?

677
00:41:18,988 --> 00:41:27,270
[SPEAKER_00]: In the two years, distinguishing the year, there was Clay Bryant, Al Milner, Greek George, they all had a big league experience.

678
00:41:29,530 --> 00:41:30,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Harley Boss.

679
00:41:30,891 --> 00:41:32,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And Eddie Morgan was there for a while.

680
00:41:33,971 --> 00:41:36,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Eddie Morgan on the way back down.

681
00:41:37,284 --> 00:41:37,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, right.

682
00:41:38,145 --> 00:41:41,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Why didn't he stick around in Warren?

683
00:41:42,127 --> 00:41:43,848
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether his...

684
00:41:44,028 --> 00:41:45,449
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a fine record?

685
00:41:45,469 --> 00:41:48,511
[SPEAKER_00]: His desire wasn't that great because he's a wealthy man, you know.

686
00:41:48,531 --> 00:41:51,433
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a big, in Canada, Louisiana, just outside in Warren.

687
00:41:51,934 --> 00:41:58,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he's retired now, but he had a, there was a box factory or something, and he's well over a millionaire.

688
00:41:58,698 --> 00:41:59,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Eddie Marvin?

689
00:41:59,378 --> 00:42:01,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

690
00:42:02,781 --> 00:42:04,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was from a wealthy family.

691
00:42:04,882 --> 00:42:06,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he inherited much of his wealth then?

692
00:42:06,765 --> 00:42:07,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, right.

693
00:42:07,445 --> 00:42:10,588
[SPEAKER_00]: He and this business, that was his inheritance.

694
00:42:10,908 --> 00:42:12,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And he ran it, and ran it well evidently.

695
00:42:13,630 --> 00:42:16,532
[SPEAKER_00]: After his own... After his baseball career was over, yes.

696
00:42:19,193 --> 00:42:20,274
[SPEAKER_00]: He was one of my heroes.

697
00:42:20,434 --> 00:42:20,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

698
00:42:20,915 --> 00:42:23,917
[SPEAKER_00]: For those Elds in Wachoma.

699
00:42:23,977 --> 00:42:24,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

700
00:42:24,937 --> 00:42:26,398
[SPEAKER_00]: This was after that, of course.

701
00:42:26,699 --> 00:42:32,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the outfielders were named Rose, Eddie Rose, Jack Ward, Pete Fleming.

702
00:42:32,683 --> 00:42:33,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Then there was a Kit Carson.

703
00:42:33,923 --> 00:42:35,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Walter Carson.

704
00:42:35,985 --> 00:42:36,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

705
00:42:36,485 --> 00:42:37,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I guess it was Walter.

706
00:42:38,545 --> 00:42:40,746
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't have called that name because there's always Kip.

707
00:42:41,706 --> 00:42:45,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And, uh... George, Chick Autry was a catcher.

708
00:42:45,827 --> 00:42:47,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Autry was up at Cleveland one time for a little while.

709
00:42:48,167 --> 00:42:49,727
[SPEAKER_00]: He was on the way back down.

710
00:42:49,747 --> 00:42:50,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, right.

711
00:42:50,287 --> 00:42:52,248
[SPEAKER_00]: He was an old, experienced guy.

712
00:42:53,808 --> 00:42:57,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And the other pitchers, one was Fred Johnson, old butterfly Fred Johnson.

713
00:42:57,749 --> 00:43:01,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Later came up with the Browns for a little while when he was probably 40 years old.

714
00:43:03,430 --> 00:43:04,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Andy Messenger.

715
00:43:05,077 --> 00:43:11,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I mentioned Al Milner, Bill Perrin was there, that's right.

716
00:43:11,861 --> 00:43:16,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Howard Craghead, was he ever down there?

717
00:43:16,664 --> 00:43:19,086
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he was a Coast League player.

718
00:43:19,506 --> 00:43:24,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Some of these fellows, we kept reading in the spring, Newton would play here every spring, they're going to be new.

719
00:43:24,569 --> 00:43:26,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Warren Johnson.

720
00:43:26,630 --> 00:43:28,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And Perrin was one of those.

721
00:43:28,512 --> 00:43:29,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Craghead was one.

722
00:43:30,052 --> 00:43:31,453
[SPEAKER_00]: I roomed with Craghead that year.

723
00:43:33,160 --> 00:43:34,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Never quite made it.

724
00:43:34,101 --> 00:43:34,421
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

725
00:43:34,461 --> 00:43:37,683
[SPEAKER_00]: We remember Walter Johnson, but we don't remember Howard Craig.

726
00:43:38,484 --> 00:43:39,044
[SPEAKER_00]: That's true.

727
00:43:39,885 --> 00:43:46,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Were they just overrated?

728
00:43:48,430 --> 00:43:50,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's hard to say.

729
00:43:50,231 --> 00:43:52,833
[SPEAKER_00]: As it turned out, you'd have to say they were overrated.

730
00:43:52,853 --> 00:43:59,638
[SPEAKER_00]: But maybe in some cases, that's not always the case.

731
00:43:59,658 --> 00:44:01,579
[SPEAKER_00]: In some cases, they could have had arm trouble.

732
00:44:01,639 --> 00:44:02,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether Craig had

733
00:44:03,371 --> 00:44:04,472
[SPEAKER_00]: had arm trouble or not.

734
00:44:05,433 --> 00:44:12,458
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know his parent did, but he may have also had arm trouble.

735
00:44:12,598 --> 00:44:18,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a hard thing to say regarding that overrated bit.

736
00:44:19,103 --> 00:44:28,510
[SPEAKER_00]: But then there's also the mental attitude of some that when they get to a certain level, they realize they're playing against players that are as good and maybe some a little better than they might be.

737
00:44:28,651 --> 00:44:31,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And if they feel that way, that's about as far as they're going to go.

738
00:44:33,504 --> 00:44:35,204
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to believe in yourself.

739
00:44:37,165 --> 00:44:40,486
[SPEAKER_00]: What were some of those ballparks like down in the Southern Lake?

740
00:44:41,026 --> 00:44:42,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Like Sulphur Dell?

741
00:44:42,506 --> 00:44:43,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Sulphur Dell was a bad one.

742
00:44:43,946 --> 00:44:48,007
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a real short right field, shorter than Lake Park in Cleveland.

743
00:44:48,027 --> 00:44:49,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Was that where they had the terraces?

744
00:44:50,048 --> 00:44:51,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, they had the terraces.

745
00:44:52,028 --> 00:44:54,329
[SPEAKER_00]: And how would you be confident enough to be able to play there?

746
00:44:54,649 --> 00:44:56,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you had to learn to play it.

747
00:44:56,169 --> 00:44:58,990
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to play and make sure.

748
00:44:59,973 --> 00:45:03,756
[SPEAKER_00]: You had to get back because you couldn't climb and then go up those steps and try to catch the ball.

749
00:45:03,776 --> 00:45:05,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So they played back against the wall.

750
00:45:06,298 --> 00:45:13,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you didn't dare come down too quick if it looked like it was low because it wasn't like running in under a ball level and getting to it.

751
00:45:14,323 --> 00:45:15,524
[SPEAKER_00]: You dropped down real quick.

752
00:45:15,965 --> 00:45:16,505
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to just

753
00:45:32,569 --> 00:45:36,490
[SPEAKER_00]: They had to just be careful not to run down and have the thing go over their head.

754
00:45:37,370 --> 00:45:40,371
[SPEAKER_00]: So it was a regular goat hill, a sight.

755
00:45:40,491 --> 00:45:44,772
[SPEAKER_00]: It was tough to pitch in.

756
00:45:45,932 --> 00:45:50,653
[SPEAKER_00]: OK, now about the pitching in Sawford Dale or these other places.

757
00:45:51,233 --> 00:45:53,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Sawford Dale was hard pitching.

758
00:45:54,094 --> 00:45:55,194
[SPEAKER_00]: I tried to pitch.

759
00:45:55,434 --> 00:45:57,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I was through pretty hard at that time.

760
00:45:57,675 --> 00:46:01,716
[SPEAKER_00]: And I tried to pitch outside to the left handers to keep them from pulling the ball down

761
00:46:03,108 --> 00:46:06,571
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you had to pitch almost inside the right-handers, too.

762
00:46:06,591 --> 00:46:12,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you had a good inside hitter, you had to pull the ball while you were in trouble there because left field wasn't too long either.

763
00:46:13,096 --> 00:46:18,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So he just made it a hard part to pitch in.

764
00:46:18,680 --> 00:46:22,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, they had a couple of pretty good hitters in Nashville when I was there.

765
00:46:22,222 --> 00:46:25,505
[SPEAKER_00]: A guy named Phil Weintraub was a good pitcher.

766
00:46:25,545 --> 00:46:29,308
[SPEAKER_00]: And Hank, boy, these names.

767
00:46:29,888 --> 00:46:30,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I can see him just as well.

768
00:46:32,883 --> 00:46:34,163
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

769
00:46:34,183 --> 00:46:36,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Lieber.

770
00:46:36,524 --> 00:46:37,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Hank Lieber.

771
00:46:37,404 --> 00:46:38,244
[SPEAKER_00]: The Giants.

772
00:46:39,065 --> 00:46:40,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

773
00:46:40,385 --> 00:46:41,445
[SPEAKER_00]: They were a couple pretty good.

774
00:46:41,465 --> 00:46:44,446
[SPEAKER_00]: They all each hit over 400 that year.

775
00:46:44,886 --> 00:46:46,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So we had problems with that.

776
00:46:47,267 --> 00:46:48,547
[SPEAKER_00]: I never realized that.

777
00:46:48,567 --> 00:46:49,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Batting average?

778
00:46:49,567 --> 00:46:49,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

779
00:46:51,328 --> 00:46:52,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Two of them over 400?

780
00:46:52,568 --> 00:46:54,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Each of them over 400, yes.

781
00:46:55,049 --> 00:46:59,690
[SPEAKER_00]: I never, I knew that some Nashville hitters had a lot of home runs.

782
00:46:59,710 --> 00:47:00,030
[SPEAKER_00]: I never,

783
00:47:00,727 --> 00:47:01,967
[SPEAKER_00]: quite tied in with that.

784
00:47:02,067 --> 00:47:06,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they got a lot of singles that bang, hit the fence, and then they couldn't go more in first base.

785
00:47:07,509 --> 00:47:15,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the ball would come back to the fielder, and they'd throw them, get the second, get back in there before they could get advanced a second, even.

786
00:47:16,350 --> 00:47:24,372
[SPEAKER_00]: But they were, they had good, I'll tell you a pitcher that they had there, Byron Speece, he was from Cleveland, the underhand, scraped his knuckles on the rubber when it threw.

787
00:47:26,073 --> 00:47:27,713
[SPEAKER_00]: He was pretty much

788
00:47:28,567 --> 00:47:31,608
[SPEAKER_00]: He was on the way down, yes, he was on the way down.

789
00:47:31,708 --> 00:47:34,409
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that was a pretty tough league.

790
00:47:34,429 --> 00:47:38,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Those guys were, some of them were just out of the big leagues, coming down.

791
00:47:43,373 --> 00:47:46,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Charlie Dressen was the manager in Nashville.

792
00:47:47,414 --> 00:47:49,115
[SPEAKER_00]: What was Pelican Stadium like?

793
00:47:50,175 --> 00:47:56,318
[SPEAKER_00]: It was shaped kind of like, almost like an arrowhead.

794
00:47:56,358 --> 00:47:57,118
[SPEAKER_00]: By that I mean,

795
00:47:57,815 --> 00:48:09,297
[SPEAKER_00]: The stands were far enough away from the foul line down to first base, and so then they cut in a little bit, and then out again, like a narrow end, right at the foul line.

796
00:48:09,317 --> 00:48:18,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So actually, the foul line was about 325 in right field, and about 315 in left field at Tulane Avenue.

797
00:48:18,739 --> 00:48:20,339
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just off left field.

798
00:48:21,139 --> 00:48:24,279
[SPEAKER_00]: And center field, it was 400 feet.

799
00:48:24,319 --> 00:48:25,220
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a nice big park.

800
00:48:25,260 --> 00:48:27,280
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't a bad park to pitch in if you could keep

801
00:48:27,816 --> 00:48:31,479
[SPEAKER_00]: player from pulling the ball, which I was fast enough to do.

802
00:48:31,499 --> 00:48:40,248
[SPEAKER_00]: There weren't too many hitters that could pull my fastball if I got it out, you know, out away from them.

803
00:48:40,348 --> 00:48:45,052
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I happened to get one inside once in a while, somebody would pull it.

804
00:48:45,072 --> 00:48:48,735
[SPEAKER_00]: But I could keep most guys from pulling.

805
00:48:49,116 --> 00:48:51,398
[SPEAKER_00]: How big a seating capacity did it have?

806
00:48:54,396 --> 00:48:56,938
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose it would seat about 9 or 10,000.

807
00:48:56,978 --> 00:49:01,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that be about average for the Southern Lake?

808
00:49:01,942 --> 00:49:09,349
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess Atlanta would have, Peachtree Stadium would have seated about 14,000.

809
00:49:09,409 --> 00:49:10,570
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be a little bit bigger.

810
00:49:10,590 --> 00:49:15,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you get some pretty big crowds down there in those depression days?

811
00:49:16,595 --> 00:49:17,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, not too bad.

812
00:49:17,816 --> 00:49:22,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, we had always had, you know, at that time the Negro bleachers were separate.

813
00:49:23,166 --> 00:49:23,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is that right?

814
00:49:23,826 --> 00:49:24,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

815
00:49:24,347 --> 00:49:26,248
[SPEAKER_00]: They couldn't come in the stands.

816
00:49:26,268 --> 00:49:34,652
[SPEAKER_00]: The Negro bleachers would be almost as many on a Sunday or holiday, the many in there, as it would be all the rest of the stands.

817
00:49:34,672 --> 00:49:36,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they had a great time at the ballgame.

818
00:49:36,973 --> 00:49:38,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Did they have their own concession stand, too?

819
00:49:39,535 --> 00:49:41,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Or was it a kind of concession stand which had one?

820
00:49:43,557 --> 00:49:44,497
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember that.

821
00:49:44,537 --> 00:49:46,819
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe they had a place where they could go.

822
00:49:47,579 --> 00:49:51,341
[SPEAKER_00]: But I believe they could have bought, I believe they could use a concession stand.

823
00:49:51,361 --> 00:49:52,962
[SPEAKER_00]: But they had to sit in the other area.

824
00:49:55,859 --> 00:50:01,422
[SPEAKER_00]: as a ball player playing in these years, did you ever wonder about the color bar?

825
00:50:04,084 --> 00:50:06,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I never gave it a whole lot of thought.

826
00:50:06,525 --> 00:50:10,588
[SPEAKER_00]: I just figured that that's just the way things were.

827
00:50:10,608 --> 00:50:20,995
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I didn't really know because they had the colored leagues, and it just seemed, from what we heard, that they were happy in their own league.

828
00:50:21,015 --> 00:50:21,835
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't want to play.

829
00:50:21,915 --> 00:50:24,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you ever barnstorm any black players?

830
00:50:25,134 --> 00:50:26,174
[SPEAKER_00]: No, never did.

831
00:50:29,056 --> 00:50:32,437
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I never saw Satchel Page till he tried to take Cleveland.

832
00:50:33,878 --> 00:50:34,978
[SPEAKER_00]: First time I ever saw him.

833
00:50:38,380 --> 00:50:46,464
[SPEAKER_00]: OK. Well, you must have been called up during the 34th season.

834
00:50:46,564 --> 00:50:47,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that right?

835
00:50:47,884 --> 00:50:52,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I was recalled at the end of the season, of course, but not to report.

836
00:50:52,126 --> 00:50:53,047
[SPEAKER_00]: I was just recalled.

837
00:50:54,530 --> 00:51:08,178
[SPEAKER_00]: because our Dixie series, by the time it was over, Cleveland of course was down in the race, there was no playoff, and they only had a week or so to play, so they just recalled me and told me to report to next spring.

838
00:51:08,318 --> 00:51:10,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Was this a pretty big thrill for you?

839
00:51:10,939 --> 00:51:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's always a thrill to be recalled by a major league club and they think you might have a chance.

840
00:51:16,803 --> 00:51:20,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you have any doubt in your own mind you were going to make it with the big list?

841
00:51:21,285 --> 00:51:22,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I always dreamt that I could.

842
00:51:24,213 --> 00:51:26,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just a matter of...

843
00:51:26,535 --> 00:51:39,506
[SPEAKER_00]: I was notoriously... Well, that's right, and then the next year at Minneapolis, yeah, but that was one of those things that I was notoriously slow starter all my life.

844
00:51:40,467 --> 00:51:42,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if they'd had...

845
00:51:42,629 --> 00:51:43,470
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel this way.

846
00:51:44,631 --> 00:51:51,937
[SPEAKER_00]: If they had had an agreement where players

847
00:51:52,955 --> 00:52:09,829
[SPEAKER_00]: from here could have gone down to Cuba or Central America in those days and played ball when I was a minor leaguer, I think I would have been able to start and get a good start in the spring by having worked all winter.

848
00:52:10,149 --> 00:52:11,350
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I was one that took

849
00:52:11,928 --> 00:52:16,251
[SPEAKER_00]: almost mid-season to get in really good shape and with a lot of work.

850
00:52:16,291 --> 00:52:18,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I didn't start out good, I wouldn't get much work.

851
00:52:18,513 --> 00:52:20,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It just took that much longer all the time.

852
00:52:20,855 --> 00:52:23,137
[SPEAKER_00]: That held me back all those years, I'm sure.

853
00:52:24,457 --> 00:52:32,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Because after the 1935, when I was optioned again, slow start in the spring, optioned to Minneapolis.

854
00:52:33,744 --> 00:52:39,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Cleveland had the Toledo Club in the league at that time, but they didn't want me to play night baseball, and Minneapolis had day baseball.

855
00:52:40,229 --> 00:52:40,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah?

856
00:52:40,810 --> 00:52:41,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

857
00:52:41,802 --> 00:52:43,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I should qualify that.

858
00:52:43,062 --> 00:52:47,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably they had, they did play a night or so a week.

859
00:52:47,424 --> 00:52:49,925
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, when we were on the road, we had to play night baseball.

860
00:52:50,445 --> 00:52:54,367
[SPEAKER_00]: But I could pitch in the daytime at home there.

861
00:52:54,427 --> 00:52:58,208
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what they wanted me to do, was work in the daytime as much as possible.

862
00:52:58,268 --> 00:52:59,129
[SPEAKER_00]: And they agreed to do that.

863
00:52:59,149 --> 00:53:01,609
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where Tony Bush was the manager when I was there.

864
00:53:02,250 --> 00:53:03,450
[SPEAKER_00]: We won the pennant that year.

865
00:53:04,010 --> 00:53:06,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you had a good year at that.

866
00:53:06,671 --> 00:53:11,053
[SPEAKER_00]: See, we won pennants or half seasons all five years.

867
00:53:11,873 --> 00:53:12,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So I was in the minors.

868
00:53:14,255 --> 00:53:25,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it was a funny thing to come up to big leagues and see the lax attitude of the players.

869
00:53:25,584 --> 00:53:26,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It just didn't matter.

870
00:53:26,865 --> 00:53:29,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody in the minors was just driving like heck.

871
00:53:29,267 --> 00:53:34,631
[SPEAKER_00]: They were driving for a job, a chance in the majors, and also to win, to get a little extra money.

872
00:53:34,892 --> 00:53:39,315
[SPEAKER_00]: In the major league, the players, I couldn't believe the attitude as compared to the minor league players.

873
00:53:41,370 --> 00:53:45,592
[SPEAKER_00]: They've got a job on the line, too, so don't they have an incentive to...

874
00:53:45,672 --> 00:53:53,976
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess they were just... their salaries were enough that it just wasn't worth it for them to bear down.

875
00:53:54,196 --> 00:53:58,699
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't have sense enough to realize what they might have done, I guess.

876
00:53:59,699 --> 00:54:00,360
[SPEAKER_00]: So many of them.

877
00:54:00,600 --> 00:54:03,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Not all of them are that way, but it's surprising how many were.

878
00:54:04,362 --> 00:54:04,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you...

879
00:54:06,818 --> 00:54:09,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Roger Peckinpah was no longer manager.

880
00:54:09,058 --> 00:54:10,099
[SPEAKER_00]: He came up, wasn't he?

881
00:54:10,179 --> 00:54:15,039
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a manager my first year I went to spring training, but not when I came up to play.

882
00:54:15,059 --> 00:54:16,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Did he get to know you very well?

883
00:54:16,740 --> 00:54:18,300
[SPEAKER_00]: I got to know Roger pretty well, yes.

884
00:54:19,120 --> 00:54:20,800
[SPEAKER_00]: I've interviewed him a couple of times.

885
00:54:21,140 --> 00:54:23,021
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't seen you for a couple of three years now.

886
00:54:23,041 --> 00:54:24,361
[SPEAKER_00]: I made a stop over this evening to see him.

887
00:54:24,441 --> 00:54:25,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm going to get a drink.

888
00:54:25,521 --> 00:54:26,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

889
00:54:26,941 --> 00:54:30,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And was Steve O'Neill or Flores?

890
00:54:30,062 --> 00:54:31,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Steve O'Neill.

891
00:54:31,262 --> 00:54:33,022
[SPEAKER_00]: I played under Walter Johnson.

892
00:54:33,042 --> 00:54:34,163
[SPEAKER_00]: I played under Steve O'Neill.

893
00:54:34,723 --> 00:54:36,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And, of course, the great Oscar Witt.

894
00:54:38,428 --> 00:54:40,489
[SPEAKER_00]: When you asked me to be my worst manager, that was it.

895
00:54:40,930 --> 00:54:41,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, go ahead.

896
00:54:41,570 --> 00:54:43,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you elaborate a little bit?

897
00:54:43,231 --> 00:54:43,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, he was your man.

898
00:54:44,692 --> 00:54:48,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, he's gone now.

899
00:54:48,114 --> 00:54:50,256
[SPEAKER_00]: But when were you under him?

900
00:54:50,296 --> 00:54:50,596
[SPEAKER_00]: 1938.

901
00:54:50,676 --> 00:54:51,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

902
00:54:53,158 --> 00:54:53,458
[SPEAKER_00]: 1938.

903
00:54:53,498 --> 00:54:55,679
[SPEAKER_00]: The last year of the Johnson.

904
00:54:55,879 --> 00:54:56,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

905
00:54:56,340 --> 00:55:07,427
[SPEAKER_00]: When he had signed up in about September of 38 for two more years, I went into the general manager's office and said, get me out of here if he's going to send me back to Myers anyway, because I don't want to play for him.

906
00:55:08,583 --> 00:55:13,708
[SPEAKER_00]: because he was just a real two-faced fella.

907
00:55:14,849 --> 00:55:18,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, it got to be a joke all around the league.

908
00:55:18,492 --> 00:55:27,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody said, as soon as I find out a player that's played for him and has a good word for him, I'll stop talking about him.

909
00:55:27,400 --> 00:55:28,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Never found anybody.

910
00:55:29,622 --> 00:55:32,084
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, he had the great year at Newark as a manager.

911
00:55:33,643 --> 00:55:37,286
[SPEAKER_00]: The players absolutely paid no attention to him.

912
00:55:37,326 --> 00:55:38,827
[SPEAKER_00]: He would try and tell them to do something.

913
00:55:38,847 --> 00:55:40,087
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd go on and win the ball games.

914
00:55:40,107 --> 00:55:42,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean that Greg Newark team was great in spite of this?

915
00:55:43,069 --> 00:55:44,290
[SPEAKER_00]: In spite of him, yeah.

916
00:55:44,310 --> 00:55:47,833
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't need to tell him or want him to do this or that.

917
00:55:47,853 --> 00:55:48,773
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd go and do their own.

918
00:55:49,514 --> 00:55:50,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Just go on and win.

919
00:55:50,134 --> 00:55:52,216
[SPEAKER_00]: They got a job in the big league because of their performance.

920
00:55:52,276 --> 00:55:53,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

921
00:55:54,017 --> 00:55:54,377
[SPEAKER_00]: He was bad.

922
00:55:54,397 --> 00:55:56,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, who didn't want him?

923
00:55:56,558 --> 00:55:58,440
[SPEAKER_00]: They had the crybaby incident later on.

924
00:55:58,500 --> 00:55:59,200
[SPEAKER_00]: I said that.

925
00:55:59,721 --> 00:56:02,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I was with the Red Sox at that time, the Red Sox.

926
00:56:02,222 --> 00:56:02,943
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd get on them.

927
00:56:03,565 --> 00:56:04,826
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know what it was like.

928
00:56:04,886 --> 00:56:07,248
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't say anything about anybody being crybaby.

929
00:56:08,248 --> 00:56:09,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I was there.

930
00:56:09,049 --> 00:56:11,291
[SPEAKER_00]: I know what it was like.

931
00:56:11,371 --> 00:56:12,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Two states.

932
00:56:12,211 --> 00:56:15,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, in a general way, can you...

933
00:56:15,454 --> 00:56:16,374
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd say one thing, too.

934
00:56:16,394 --> 00:56:19,176
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't turn around and tell somebody else just the opposite about you.

935
00:56:20,077 --> 00:56:23,319
[SPEAKER_00]: That, plus the fact that he was always showing somebody up.

936
00:56:23,339 --> 00:56:26,942
[SPEAKER_00]: He coached third base, you know.

937
00:56:27,122 --> 00:56:31,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And then somebody, maybe, happened to bunt a ball foul.

938
00:56:31,105 --> 00:56:32,546
[SPEAKER_00]: He turned and said, what can I do?

939
00:56:32,626 --> 00:56:32,926
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

940
00:56:33,462 --> 00:56:35,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Give them to the fans that, what can I do to deliver?

941
00:56:36,063 --> 00:56:36,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

942
00:56:37,584 --> 00:56:37,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

943
00:56:37,944 --> 00:56:38,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Give it to the fans.

944
00:56:38,964 --> 00:56:39,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sure.

945
00:56:41,325 --> 00:56:41,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

946
00:56:42,706 --> 00:56:49,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And then on the bench, when, like, Feller was, the first year, it was 36, and then 37, 38.

947
00:56:50,189 --> 00:56:53,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Feller was, he was still wild in 38, you know.

948
00:56:54,731 --> 00:56:57,592
[SPEAKER_00]: But famous, and then winning quite a lot of ballgames.

949
00:56:58,392 --> 00:57:03,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And he'd have a wild streak, and walk around in, maybe, and bid to pace up a dollar.

950
00:57:04,008 --> 00:57:04,868
[SPEAKER_00]: What can I do?

951
00:57:05,268 --> 00:57:08,169
[SPEAKER_00]: They're supposed to be my ace, and he's out there like a pusher.

952
00:57:09,230 --> 00:57:11,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the way he talked all the time.

953
00:57:11,070 --> 00:57:11,770
[SPEAKER_00]: It just made the...

954
00:57:11,790 --> 00:57:12,931
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just terrible.

955
00:57:14,071 --> 00:57:15,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So I had no love for Oscar.

956
00:57:15,671 --> 00:57:16,011
[SPEAKER_00]: I had none.

957
00:57:18,352 --> 00:57:19,132
[SPEAKER_00]: He was just a...

958
00:57:19,873 --> 00:57:20,793
[SPEAKER_00]: He had no baseball?

959
00:57:22,573 --> 00:57:24,294
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess if he...

960
00:57:25,954 --> 00:57:26,575
[SPEAKER_00]: He could...

961
00:57:26,695 --> 00:57:27,695
[SPEAKER_00]: The percentages...

962
00:57:27,715 --> 00:57:31,516
[SPEAKER_00]: He was pretty well versed in the percentages, but, of course, when it come right down to it,

963
00:57:31,815 --> 00:57:35,216
[SPEAKER_00]: There was always a certain suspicion that he didn't run the team completely.

964
00:57:36,017 --> 00:57:37,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Mrs. Vitt did a lot of it.

965
00:57:38,918 --> 00:57:41,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't mean to imply that the general manager or anything.

966
00:57:41,719 --> 00:57:44,540
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what they, how their things were.

967
00:57:44,560 --> 00:57:48,142
[SPEAKER_00]: But Mrs. Vitt, if she didn't like a player, he didn't play too much.

968
00:57:49,062 --> 00:57:49,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

969
00:57:50,683 --> 00:57:51,583
[SPEAKER_00]: It was kind of funny.

970
00:57:53,884 --> 00:57:57,606
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, she was among the, she'd be around the wives and make some remarks.

971
00:57:57,686 --> 00:57:59,607
[SPEAKER_00]: And of course, the wives didn't know all those things.

